![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

In shell scripts with `set -u` or `set -euo pipefail` enabled, referencing potentially unset variables directly (such as `$session_key`, `$work_dir`, `$title`, `$prompt`, and `$prompt_file`) will cause an unbound variable error and terminate the script. Use parameter expansion `${VAR:-}` to safely check if the variables are empty or unset before performing file or other checks.

```shell
_validate_run_args() {
	[[ -n "${session_key:-}" ]] || {
		print_error "run requires --session-key"
		return 1
	}
	[[ -n "${work_dir:-}" ]] || {
		print_error "run requires --dir"
		return 1
	}
	[[ -n "${title:-}" ]] || {
		print_error "run requires --title"
		return 1
	}
	if [[ -z "${prompt:-}" && -n "${prompt_file:-}" ]]; then
		[[ -f "${prompt_file:-}" ]] || {
			print_error "Prompt file not found: ${prompt_file:-}"
			return 1
		}
		prompt=$(<"${prompt_file:-}")
	fi
	[[ -n "${prompt:-}" ]] || {
		print_error "run requires --prompt or --prompt-file"
		return 1
	}
	return 0
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. In Shell scripts with set -u or set -euo pipefail enabled, referencing a potentially unset variable directly will cause an unbound variable error and terminate the script. Use parameter expansion ${VAR:-} to safely check if the variable is empty or unset.
</details>

